Chapter: BrotliTest
  cites Compress chapter Brotli

 Compress, decompress, verify byte identity -- and verify the output is SMALLER
 on run-heavy input, the assertion a pass-through cannot pass.

 brotli-compress emitted only stored meta-blocks and inflated everything.
 RLE meta-blocks now give real compression on runs. A round-trip alone cannot
 tell a compressor from a pipe, so the size assertion runs on a compressible
 input: two long runs. The mixed input exercises the stored-block fallback and
 the RLE/stored boundary in one round-trip.

Section: Helpers

  list-equal : List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  list-equal (a) (b) (i) (len) =
    if i >= len then True
    else if list-at a i == list-at b i then list-equal a b (i + 1) len
    else False

  check-roundtrip : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-roundtrip (input) (label) =
    let compressed = brotli-compress input
    in let decompressed = brotli-decompress compressed
    in let ok = if list-length decompressed == list-length input then list-equal input decompressed 0 (list-length input) else False
    in label & "=" & if ok then "pass" else "fail"

  check-smaller : List Integer, Text -> Text
  check-smaller (input) (label) =
    let compressed = brotli-compress input
    in label & "=" & if list-length compressed < list-length input then "pass" else "fail"

  run-of : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  run-of (v) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else run-of v n (i + 1) (list-push acc v)

Section: Body

  test-empty : List Integer = []
  test-small : List Integer = [72, 101, 108, 108, 111]

 Two runs of three hundred bytes: long enough that the header does not swamp
 the win, and pure runs are exactly what RLE meta-blocks encode.

  test-runs : List Integer = run-of 66 300 0 (run-of 65 300 0 [])

 Literals, then a run, then literals: exercises stored fallback and both block
 boundaries in a single round-trip.

  test-mixed : List Integer = [1, 2, 3] & run-of 9 20 0 [] & [4, 5, 6]

 A six-byte cycle repeated a hundred times: no run of four identical bytes, so
 RLE never fires, but LZ77 matches it heavily. An RLE-and-stored-only Brotli
 stores this verbatim and comes out LARGER; the compressed meta-block (a Deflate
 block) shrinks it. brotli-cyclic-smaller is the assertion that fails without a
 real entropy coder.

  test-cyclic : List Integer = cyc 600 0 []

  cyc : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  cyc (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else cyc n (i + 1) (list-push acc (65 + (i - (i / 6) * 6)))

 A skewed alphabet reaching past byte 128, which the Huffman literal code should
 shrink well. There is no magic number in RFC 7932, so brotli-valid can only say
 the input could be a stream we wrote -- an empty input is the one thing it can
 honestly reject, and asserting more than that is asserting a property the format
 does not have.

  test-high : List Integer = hi 900 0 []

  hi : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  hi (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else hi n (i + 1) (list-push acc (if i - (i / 5) * 5 == 0 then 200 + (i - (i / 11) * 11) else 213))

 An order-one input, for context modelling. A letter is always followed by a
 punctuation byte and a punctuation byte always by a letter, but WHICH one is
 driven by a linear congruential state, so there is nothing for the matcher to
 find and the marginal distribution over the fifty-two values is flat. A single
 literal code must therefore spend the full order-zero entropy on every byte;
 codes chosen by the previous byte spend the order-one entropy instead.

 The two classes are 65..90 and 32..57, which is deliberate: context mode zero is
 the previous byte's low SIX bits, so the classes have to stay disjoint under that
 mask or the contexts collide and no clustering can separate them. 128..159 would
 have looked equally reasonable and mapped onto 0..31, straight through the
 letters.

 The size assertion is what makes this a test of the feature rather than of the
 round-trip. Measured both ways on this input: 1742 bytes with a single literal
 code, 1460 with context modelling. The threshold sits between them, so the case
 fails if the feature stops firing and fails if it never fired.

  test-ctx : List Integer = ctxseq 2400 0 1 32 []

  ctx-lcg : Integer -> Integer
  ctx-lcg (s) =
    let t = s * 1103515245 + 12345
    in t - (t / 2147483648) * 2147483648

  ctxseq : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  ctxseq (n) (i) (s) (prev) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let s2 = ctx-lcg s
    in let h = s2 / 65536
    in let d = h - (h / 26) * 26
    in let b = if prev >= 65 then 32 + d else 65 + d
    in ctxseq n (i + 1) s2 b (list-push acc b)

 A near-flat literal code, for code 16. This input spreads its bytes almost
 evenly, so almost every symbol ends up at the same code length and the
 description becomes one long run of equal non-zero lengths -- which is exactly
 what code 16 collapses and code 17 cannot touch.

 Measured both ways on this input: 624 bytes without code 16, 548 with. The
 threshold sits between them, so the case fails if the feature stops firing and
 would have failed if it had never fired.

  test-flat : List Integer = br16-of 1200 0 []

  br16-byte : Integer -> Integer
  br16-byte (i) =
    let v = i * 97 + (i / 7) * 31 + 11
    in v - (v / 256) * 256

  br16-of : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  br16-of (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else br16-of n (i + 1) (list-push acc (br16-byte i))

 Prose, for block splitting. Words drawn from a small vocabulary by a carried
 linear congruential state -- carried rather than indexed, so the text does not
 become periodic and collapse into a single long match. That gives MANY short
 commands, which is what block splitting needs: an input the matcher turns into
 two or three long copies has nothing to cut.

 A cycle-and-spread pair was tried here first and pinned nothing. The matcher
 reduced the cyclic half to a couple of long copies and the flat half to one long
 insert, leaving about three commands in total, and the costing rightly declined
 to split three commands. It measured 573 bytes both ways -- a test that cannot
 fail, and the reason this input is prose instead.

 Measured both ways: 1466 bytes with one block type, 601 with two.

  test-blk : List Integer = bw-text 3000 1 []

 Long enough that the distance codes split into two blocks. Three thousand bytes
 is not: a second distance code has to pay for its own description, a context map
 and a switch, and only around nine thousand does it start returning more than
 that. This is the only round trip that reads a stream carrying more than one
 distance block type, so it is the only one that can see the reader lose the
 boundary.

  test-dsp : List Integer = bw-text 9000 7 []

  bw-word : Integer -> List Integer
  bw-word (k) =
    if k == 0 then [116, 104, 101]
    else if k == 1 then [113, 117, 105, 99, 107]
    else if k == 2 then [98, 114, 111, 119, 110]
    else if k == 3 then [102, 111, 120]
    else if k == 4 then [106, 117, 109, 112, 115]
    else if k == 5 then [111, 118, 101, 114]
    else if k == 6 then [108, 97, 122, 121]
    else if k == 7 then [100, 111, 103]
    else [97, 110, 100]

  bw-text : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  bw-text (n) (s) (acc) =
    if list-length acc >= n then acc
    else let s2 = ctx-lcg s
    in let k = (s2 / 4096) - ((s2 / 4096) / 9) * 9
    in bw-text n s2 (acc & bw-word k & [32])

 The nine insert-and-copy group bases, pinned. Each of these symbol values was
 MEASURED against .NET's BrotliStream by building a stream that uses it and
 keeping what the oracle accepted, and the arrangement is irregular: 448 sits
 where the table's shape suggests 512, and the group depends on the copy code as
 well as the insert code.

 This is pinned here rather than left to the interop probe because a wrong base
 is only caught when a command actually LANDS in that group, and the far groups
 need an insert of 130-plus bytes followed by a long copy. An input that happens
 not to reach group (2,2) would leave a wrong 640 sitting there passing every
 test. Reading the table directly cannot miss it.

  ic-case : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  ic-case (ins-c) (cpy-c) (want) (label) =
    label & "=" & if brotli-ic-sym ins-c cpy-c == want then "pass" else "fail"

  ic-back : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  ic-back (sym) (ins-c) (cpy-c) (label) =
    label & "=" & if brotli-ic-ins-code sym == ins-c then
      (if brotli-ic-cpy-code sym == cpy-c then "pass" else "fail")
    else "fail"

 Ten words from RFC 7932's own corpus, repeated: the shape the static dictionary
 exists for. Written as literal ASCII rather than read out of our own corpus,
 because generating the input from BrotliDict would make the test circular -- a
 corpus that drifted would produce a matching input and still agree with itself.

 THE THRESHOLD SITS BETWEEN TWO MEASURED NUMBERS. With the dictionary off (its
 floor raised above the longest word) this input is 114 bytes; with it on, 64. A
 pin at 90 therefore fails if the dictionary stops firing AND would have failed
 if it had never fired, which a ratio that merely does not move cannot tell you.

